---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Vakantieteller publisher: who we are and our sources';
const description = 'Who publishes Vakantieteller: Radif Partners, independent and selling no travel. Where the school calendars, public holidays and Easter dates come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Since French-language schools reformed their calendar in 2022, Belgium has had three school calendars that differ on five points. The usual lists give a row of dates per Community, but seldom put them side by side, never count the days two children are off together and mix up school breaks with public holidays. This site lines the three calendars up, counts down to the next break and links them to the public holidays, replacement days and bridge days of working parents.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not an authority, a school network or a lobby group, has no link with any Community or ministry and uses no official logos. The site sells no trips, stays or calendars and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>The Flemish breaks are on <Fragment set:html={s('vg-vakanties', 'vlaanderen.be')} />, the French-language ones on <Fragment set:html={s('fwb-calendrier', 'enseignement.be')} />, and the German-speaking ones in the <Fragment set:html={s('dg-kalender', 'government’s published calendar')} />. Public holidays come from the <Fragment set:html={s('wet-1974', 'Act of 4 January 1974')} /> and the guidance of the <Fragment set:html={s('fod-feestdagen', 'federal employment service')} />; Easter from <Fragment set:html={s('ostern-ptb', 'Gauss’s formula')} />. How everything is computed is on the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a> page; how we correct mistakes is in the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>Every tool runs in your browser. What you choose is neither sent nor stored. There is no account and no form. Details are in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy notice</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not know your school’s in-service days and decides nothing about your leave. Whether 11 July, 27 September or 24 December is a day off for you is set by your work rules or sector agreement.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a break, a public holiday or a date looks wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
